        "difficulty": "easy",
        "description": "<p class=\"\" data-start=\"23\" data-end=\"60\">Duggan Corporation, a leading tech firm, is recruiting Software Engineers (SWEs) from a pool of applicants. Their HR team has compiled candidate data, including academic performance and problem-solving skills, to identify top talent. You are given a table: 'candidate'.</p>\n<ul data-start=\"62\" data-end=\"345\">\n<li class=\"\" data-start=\"62\" data-end=\"345\">\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"64\" data-end=\"345\">The 'candidate' table includes the columns 'id', 'name', 'cgpa', 'problem_solved', and 'education'. Each row represents a candidate with their academic background and competitive problem-solving record. The 'education' column can have values 'Diploma', 'UG', or 'PG'.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"363\">Your task is to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"366\" data-end=\"475\">Filter out candidates who have a CGPA of <strong data-start=\"407\" data-end=\"423\">at least 8.0</strong> and whose education is either <strong data-start=\"454\" data-end=\"462\">'UG'</strong> or <strong data-start=\"466\" data-end=\"474\">'PG'</strong>.</li>\n<li data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"576\">Rank the remaining candidates in descending order of 'problem_solved'. In case of a tie in 'problem_solved', rank them by higher 'cgpa'.</li>\n<li data-start=\"579\" data-end=\"629\">Return the <strong data-start=\"590\" data-end=\"610\">top 5 candidates</strong> based on the rank.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"\" data-start=\"631\" data-end=\"802\">The final output should include the columns 'id', 'name', 'cgpa', 'problem_solved', ordered by the number of problems solved in descending order.</p>",
